# Behaviour fixture for the `agent-router` alerts (homelab#332; FU-158's behaviour half, the
# #288/#313 restart-gap class in its third and fourth alert).
#
#   devbox run prometheus-rules-lint      # runs this, and every other *.promtool-test under argocd/
#   .devbox/nix/profile/default/bin/promtool test rules agent-router.promtool-test   # from this dir
#
# `promtool check rules` proves the exprs PARSE; this proves they behave — above all ACROSS A
# PROXY ROLL, which is the whole point of #332. CI-enforced as of 2026-08-11 (prometheus-rules-lint
# is inside the required `ci` check), so unlike #288's fixture this one does not need running by
# hand to matter.
#
# WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS NOT. This file loads ONLY the shipped rules, so it asserts what the
# deployed alerts do. The pre-#332 contrast lives in agent-router-witness.promtool-{rules,test} —
# forced apart, not a style choice: promtool evaluates every rule in every loaded file at every
# step, and the pre-#332 OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow expr errors out whenever two series share a `key`,
# which is exactly the input §MASKING below needs. That header has the detail.
#
# Named .promtool-test, not .yaml: manifest-lint kubeconforms every *.yaml under argocd/resources,
# and a non-manifest there would fail it.
#
# CLOCK: promtool's test clock starts at 0. `router_rotation_age_seconds` is an AGE in seconds, so
# the values below start just over the 259200s (3d) threshold and climb 60/minute — the same rate
# real time moves, because the proxy computes it as `now - MAX(updated_ts)` against the persisted
# `rotation` table. Only the comparison against 259200 is asserted on, which is what the alert does.
#
# LABELS are the real scraped set, read off the live series on 2026-08-11 and reused verbatim from
# subscription.promtool-test: container, endpoint, instance, job, namespace, pod, service, plus the
# metric's own `source` / `key`. `pod` and `instance` are the two that churn on a roll. The `key`
# values are the real ref shape — `<namespace>/<secret>`, e.g. the proxy's own
# `agent-egress/router-account-key` (deployment.yaml `ROUTER_ACCOUNT_REF`).
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT MODEL, and why it is still conclusive: promtool has no STALENESS
# MARKER. In production the old pod's series stops being readable the instant its target goes
# away; here a series that simply ends stays readable for the full 5m PromQL lookback. So every
# silence measured below is a LOWER BOUND on the production silence, and holes are modelled LONGER
# than the ones actually measured (69-116s of raw samples across 10 pod generations) so that the
# 5m lookback stops hiding them. A fixture that under-states the bug and still catches it is the
# honest direction to err in — subscription.promtool-test's §Shape 1 note, unchanged here.
rule_files:
  - agent-router.promtool-rules

evaluation_interval: 1m

tests:
  # ══ RouterRotationStale — the PERSISTED arm ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

  # ── R1 — a roll mid-staleness does not cost the 1h window ────────────────────────────────────
  # The rankings feed has been dead for 3d+ and the alert is firing. router.py is edited, the
  # ConfigMap re-hashes, the single replica is replaced. The `rotation` table is on the PVC, so the
  # new pod computes the SAME staleness off the SAME row and the age keeps climbing — the condition
  # never stopped being true, only the series did.
  - interval: 1m
    name: R1 rotation — a 2m roll mid-staleness leaves the firing alert untouched
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.71:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-8574bd8d9-r42tx",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "259500+60x70"
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.83:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-cdc58fc45-wm8kr",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "_x72 263820+60x70"
    alert_rule_test:
      # `for: 1h` is honoured — 59m in, the condition is true and nothing has fired.
      - eval_time: 59m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []
      # 61m: firing. Note the label set — `max by (source)` is half of the fix, so the alert's
      # identity no longer contains `pod`/`instance` and a new pod cannot mint a new instance.
      - eval_time: 61m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 1h 6m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."
      # ── THE ROLL: last sample of r42tx at 70m, first of wm8kr at 72m ─────────────────────────
      # 75m is inside the [10m] range window, so BOTH pod generations are resolvable at this one
      # instant — and there is still exactly ONE alert, because `max by (source)` collapses them.
      # (That collapse is a nicety here and load-bearing for OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow below, where
      # two series sharing a match label is an error rather than a duplicate.)
      - eval_time: 75m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 1h 20m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."
      # 130m — long past 72m+1h, so a reset at the roll would still be silent here. It is not: this
      # is the same alert instance that went active at 60m, and the age has kept climbing through.
      - eval_time: 130m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 2h 15m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."

  # ── R2 — THE CEILING, asserted rather than assumed ───────────────────────────────────────────
  # [10m] is a bridge, not immunity. A hole longer than the range still resets the window, and
  # pretending otherwise is how a "fixed" alert surprises someone later. A 13m hole (crash-loop,
  # cold registry-mirror image pull, node drain, Longhorn-slow attach) costs the full 1h again.
  # Widening the range is not free either — it is also how long a resolved condition keeps ringing
  # (§R3). This is the same ceiling #313 asserted, at this alert's longer `for:`.
  - interval: 1m
    name: R2 rotation — a 13m hole DOES reset the 1h window, the accepted limit of [10m]
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.90:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ccc-3",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "259500+60x70"
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.91:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ddd-4",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "_x83 264480+60x110"
    alert_rule_test:
      # firing before the hole, exactly as in R1
      - eval_time: 61m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 1h 6m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."
      # 81m: the range window (71m…81m] holds no sample at all — the alert resolves.
      - eval_time: 81m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []
      # and it re-fires a full hour after the new pod's first sample (83m + 1h), not before
      - eval_time: 142m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 144m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 2h 29m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."

  # ── R3 — the price of [10m], paid on the way down ────────────────────────────────────────────
  # `max` over the range means the WORST reading in the last 10m wins, so once the rankings pull
  # lands and the age collapses to ~0 the alert keeps ringing until the stale samples fall out of
  # the window. Asserted so the trade is a fact in a test rather than a claim in a comment. Against
  # `for: 1h` on a 3-day staleness threshold, ≤10m of over-ring is not worth a narrower range.
  - interval: 1m
    name: R3 rotation — after the feed recovers, the alert clears within the [10m] window
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.92:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-eee-5",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "259500+60x70 0+60x60"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 61m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 1h 6m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."
      # the pull lands at 71m (age → 0); at 75m the stale samples are still inside (65m…75m].
      # The rendered age is 3d 1h 15m — the reading from 70m, the last moment it was true — where
      # R1 rendered 3d 1h 20m at this same eval time. That gap IS the stickiness, made legible:
      # a page delivered here quotes the pre-recovery number, which is the honest thing for it to
      # say and another reason not to widen [10m].
      - eval_time: 75m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              source: openrouter-daily-rankings
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter rankings rotation feed is stale (>3d)"
              description: "The dailyish list-daily-model-rankings pull has not written rotation entries for 3d 1h 15m 0s — check proxy logs for MCP/Cloudflare failures (the UA gotcha) and the router-account-key Secret."
      # gone by 81m — ≤10m after recovery, and it needs no new `for:` window to clear
      - eval_time: 81m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []

  # ── R4 — the guards: a live feed, and the other rotation sources ─────────────────────────────
  # `source` is a real dimension, not decoration: router.py records `provider-events` and
  # `scout-canary` into the same table. Only the daily-rankings feed is alerted on, and the
  # matcher — not the aggregation — is what keeps the others out. `max by (source)` would happily
  # report the worst of all three if the matcher were dropped.
  - interval: 1m
    name: R4 rotation — a fresh rankings feed is silent even while another source is ancient
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.93:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-fff-6",service="openrouter-proxy",source="openrouter-daily-rankings"}'
        values: "3600+60x140"
      - series: 'router_rotation_age_seconds{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.93:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-fff-6",service="openrouter-proxy",source="provider-events"}'
        values: "900000+60x140"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 61m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 140m
        alertname: RouterRotationStale
        exp_alerts: []

  # ══ OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow — the EXTERNALLY-TRUE arm, and the `on (key)` match ════════════════

  # ── K1 — a roll does not cost the 30m window, and the hole here is the biggest in the file ───
  # $8 left against a $100 limit: 8% and falling, the alert firing correctly. Then the proxy rolls.
  # These two series are slower to come back than any others on this target — `_headroom_loop`
  # sleeps 75s after start before its first auth/key tick, on top of the pod gap, the ≤15s call
  # and a 60s scrape — so ≈4.5m is the realistic worst case. Modelled at 7m: longer than production
  # (so the 5m lookback cannot hide it from the witness fixture's copy of this shape) and still
  # inside [10m], which is the margin this alert actually has.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K1 key budget — a 7m roll hole leaves the firing alert untouched, both sides bridged
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.71:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-8574bd8d9-r42tx",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "8+0x40"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.71:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-8574bd8d9-r42tx",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x40"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.83:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-cdc58fc45-wm8kr",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x47 8+0x80"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.83:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-cdc58fc45-wm8kr",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x47 100+0x80"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 29m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      # 31m: firing. The label set is `{key}` and nothing else — and it ALREADY was before #332,
      # because a one-to-one `/ on (key)` reduces the result to the match labels. Identity churn
      # was never this alert's failure mode; the hole was.
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      # ── THE ROLL: last sample at 40m, first of the new pod at 47m ────────────────────────────
      # 46m is inside the hole with even the 5m lookback exhausted — the RAW gauge is unreadable
      # here. The alert fires anyway, and the description still renders "8 USD" because the
      # annotation's own `query` carries the same [10m] bridge. Unbridged it would have read
      # "has  USD left" on a page sent during a roll.
      - eval_time: 46m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      # 60m — past 47m+30m, so a reset at the roll would still be silent here. Exactly one alert,
      # which is the assertion that stands in for the un-assertable one: at 48-57m BOTH pod
      # generations are inside the [10m] range, and it is `max by (key)` that turns them into one
      # series. Without it `on (key)` raises a many-to-one error and this returns nothing at all.
      - eval_time: 60m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."

  # ── K2 — a gap on the REMAINING side alone ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Not a roll: same pod throughout. OpenRouter's auth/key can answer with a `limit` and a null
  # `limit_remaining`, and openrouter-proxy.py emits each field only when it is a number — so one
  # side of the match can vanish while the other keeps scraping. Under `on (key)` that emptied the
  # WHOLE expression, which is why "bridge one side" was never a partial fix.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K2 key budget — a 9m gap on the remaining side alone does not reset the window
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.94:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ggg-7",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "8+0x40 _x8 8+0x72"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.94:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ggg-7",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x120"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      # 47m: the raw numerator is gone (last sample 40m, lookback spent) — bridged, so still firing
      - eval_time: 47m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      - eval_time: 60m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."

  # ── K3 — a gap on the LIMIT side alone ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The mirror of K2, and the reason both are here: `on (key)` is symmetric, so a one-sided fix
  # would pass one of these two cases and fail the other. `limit` is the field OpenRouter omits
  # entirely for an unlimited key, so this side blanking is the likelier of the two in practice.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K3 key budget — a 9m gap on the limit side alone does not reset the window either
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.95:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-hhh-8",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "8+0x120"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.95:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-hhh-8",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x40 _x8 100+0x72"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      - eval_time: 47m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      - eval_time: 60m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."

  # ── K4 — THE CEILING again, on the alert that has the least margin for it ────────────────────
  # Same accepted limit as R2. Worth asserting separately here because this alert's real-world
  # hole is the ≈4.5m one (the 75s headroom-poller settle), so the distance to the ceiling is
  # ~2x, not the ~5x #288 measured — the number that would have to change first if the poller's
  # startup sleep ever grew.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K4 key budget — a 13m hole DOES reset the 30m window
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.96:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-iii-9",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "8+0x40"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.96:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-iii-9",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x40"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.97:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-jjj-10",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x53 8+0x80"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.97:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-jjj-10",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x53 100+0x80"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."
      # 51m: the range window (41m…51m] is empty on both sides — resolved
      - eval_time: 51m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      # re-fires 30m after the new pod's first sample (53m + 30m), not before
      - eval_time: 82m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 84m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."

  # ── K5 — MASKING: the trade `max by (key)` accepts, executed instead of argued ───────────────
  # Aggregating away identity means one healthy instance hides a low one. Here two replicas report
  # the SAME key, one seeing $8 of the $100 limit and one seeing $50 (a staler auth/key poll, the
  # realistic way they would disagree). `max` on the numerator takes the optimistic $50 → 50%, and
  # the alert stays silent even though a real replica is at 8%.
  # UNREACHABLE TODAY: openrouter-proxy is `replicas: 1` with `strategy: Recreate`, so two live
  # generations never overlap in the raw vector. This case is the tripwire for the day that
  # changes — scaling out means flipping the numerator to `min by (key) (min_over_time(...[10m]))`
  # first, and this assertion is what will have to be rewritten to notice. Note the asymmetry with
  # RouterRotationStale: there `max` takes the WORST reading of an age, so no masking is possible.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K5 key budget — MASKING, the accepted cost of aggregating identity away
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.98:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-kkk-11",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "8+0x120"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.98:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-kkk-11",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x120"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.99:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-lll-12",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "50+0x120"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.2.99:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-lll-12",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x120"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 120m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []

  # ── K6 — a healthy key is silent, and the [10m] onset delay is priced ────────────────────────
  # The same `max`-is-optimistic property in the TIME dimension, which unlike K5 IS reachable at
  # one replica: when remaining drops off a cliff, the pre-drop reading stays in the range window
  # and holds the alert off for up to 10m. Against `for: 30m` on a budget that drains over days,
  # a ≤10m later page is the cheaper side of the trade — but it is a real cost, so it is asserted.
  - interval: 1m
    name: K6 key budget — healthy is silent, and a sudden drop pages ~10m later than it would have
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_remaining_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.10:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-mmm-13",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "50+0x40 8+0x80"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_key_limit_usd{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.10:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",key="agent-egress/router-account-key",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-mmm-13",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "100+0x120"
    alert_rule_test:
      # 50% of the limit: nowhere near the threshold
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      # the drop is at 41m; the un-bridged expr would have gone pending there and fired at 71m
      - eval_time: 72m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      # the bridged one goes pending at 50m — when the last $50 sample leaves (40m…50m] — and
      # fires at 80m. Nine minutes late, and the reason the range is [10m] and not [30m].
      - eval_time: 79m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 81m
        alertname: OpenRouterKeyBudgetLow
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              key: agent-egress/router-account-key
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter key agent-egress/router-account-key below 10% of its spend limit"
              description: "Standing key agent-egress/router-account-key has 8 USD left under its limit — OpenRouter-rail dispatch for that project will die on 402s when it empties."

  # ══ The three that must NOT be bridged — "deliberately unchanged", executed ═══════════════════
  # These two cases exist so the asymmetry is a running assertion rather than a comment. If a
  # future sweep re-applies the #288 recipe here on the "single replica + `for:`" surface alone,
  # these fail. (SubscriptionDispatchLimited, the third, is in the `claude-subscription` group and
  # is covered by subscription.promtool-test — untouched by #332.)

  # ── M1 — OpenRouterCapacityDown: the roll DOES clear it, and that is correct ─────────────────
  # The latch is in-memory (`_or_cap`). A fresh pod has never seen a 429 and honestly reports 0, so
  # the alert resolving at the roll is the alert telling the truth. Bridging it would page for a
  # latch that no longer exists anywhere. The known cost — a ~30m re-arm blank if the outage is
  # still going — is already priced in the manifest comment (homelab#162 review).
  - interval: 1m
    name: M1 capacity latch — a roll clears it and it does not come back on its own
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_openrouter_capacity_down{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.11:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-nnn-14",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "1+0x40"
      - series: 'router_openrouter_capacity_down{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.12:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ooo-15",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x43 0+0x80"
    alert_rule_test:
      # firing under the OLD pod's identity — `pod`/`instance` are on this alert, deliberately
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: OpenRouterCapacityDown
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              triage: none
              container: proxy
              endpoint: http
              instance: 10.244.3.11:8080
              job: openrouter-proxy
              namespace: agent-egress
              pod: openrouter-proxy-nnn-14
              service: openrouter-proxy
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter account is capacity-down"
              description: 'OpenRouter account-scope capacity has been latched for 30m — worker dispatch is degrading to the haiku subscription rail or deferring the OpenRouter rail. Probe: GET openrouter-proxy.agent-egress:8080/router-status → .openrouter_capacity (reason / remaining_s / recent_429_models) names which leg latched. The credit leg reads the openrouter-operator''s openrouter_account_credit_usd gauge (openrouter-operator-metrics.openrouter-operator:9090), NOT OpenRouter directly — .credit_poll_failures climbing there, or router_openrouter_credit_poll_failures_total here, means that leg is blind and only the data-plane legs (hard-402 / cross-model 429s) can still fire (homelab#180).'
      - eval_time: 50m
        alertname: OpenRouterCapacityDown
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 120m
        alertname: OpenRouterCapacityDown
        exp_alerts: []

  # ── M2 — RouterDbEphemeral: a persisting fault re-pays its 30m after the roll ────────────────
  # `router_db_persistent` is THIS pod's PVC-open result. The volume is still broken, so the new
  # pod fails the open too and reads 0 — but it is a NEW measurement, with its own window. That
  # 30m blank is the honest cost of not bridging, and it is bounded: the fault re-pages.
  - interval: 1m
    name: M2 ephemeral store — the roll costs 30m and the alert returns under the new pod
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_db_persistent{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.13:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ppp-16",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "0+0x40"
      - series: 'router_db_persistent{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.14:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-qqq-17",service="openrouter-proxy"}'
        values: "_x43 0+0x90"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: RouterDbEphemeral
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              container: proxy
              endpoint: http
              instance: 10.244.3.13:8080
              job: openrouter-proxy
              namespace: agent-egress
              pod: openrouter-proxy-ppp-16
              service: openrouter-proxy
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "Router store is running ephemeral (:memory:)"
              description: "The openrouter-proxy router store failed to open its PVC and degraded to :memory: — strikes, provider events, generation costs and the persisted 429 latch are lost on every restart. Check the openrouter-proxy-data PVC / Longhorn volume in ns agent-egress."
      # the 30m blank
      - eval_time: 50m
        alertname: RouterDbEphemeral
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 72m
        alertname: RouterDbEphemeral
        exp_alerts: []
      # 43m + 30m — back, under the new pod's identity
      - eval_time: 74m
        alertname: RouterDbEphemeral
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              container: proxy
              endpoint: http
              instance: 10.244.3.14:8080
              job: openrouter-proxy
              namespace: agent-egress
              pod: openrouter-proxy-qqq-17
              service: openrouter-proxy
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "Router store is running ephemeral (:memory:)"
              description: "The openrouter-proxy router store failed to open its PVC and degraded to :memory: — strikes, provider events, generation costs and the persisted 429 latch are lost on every restart. Check the openrouter-proxy-data PVC / Longhorn volume in ns agent-egress."

  # ══ ADR-107 flip-acceptance 1 (homelab#515): the requested≠served drift belt ───────────────────
  # Both counters are COMPUTED from the PVC-backed store at scrape time (router.py
  # model_drift_rows), so a roll re-computes them from the same rows — the condition outlives
  # the roll and the max_over_time([10m]) bridge is what the two alerts need (the #288/#313
  # single-replica class, persisted arm). These cases assert the bridge holds AND the honest
  # split between drift (served evidence exists but differs) and unverifiable (absent served
  # side — absence never reads as agreement).

  # ── D1 — a concrete drift count fires, and the [10m] bridge survives the roll ────────────────
  # OpenRouter ride requested deepseek-v4-flash but the /generation harvest shows it served
  # kimi-k3. router_run_model_drift_total is 1 on the PVC-backed store; a roll re-computes the
  # same 1 from the same rows (the persisted arm).
  - interval: 1m
    name: D1 drift — a requested≠served count fires and survives a roll
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_run_model_drift_total{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.15:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-rrr-18",service="openrouter-proxy",rail="openrouter",stack="sleep",role="worker",requested="deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",served="moonshotai/kimi-k3",provider="Moonshot"}'
        values: "1+0x40"
      - series: 'router_run_model_drift_total{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.16:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-sss-19",service="openrouter-proxy",rail="openrouter",stack="sleep",role="worker",requested="deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash",served="moonshotai/kimi-k3",provider="Moonshot"}'
        values: "_x43 1+0x80"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 29m
        alertname: RouterRunModelDrift
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: RouterRunModelDrift
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              rail: openrouter
              stack: sleep
              role: worker
              requested: deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash
              served: moonshotai/kimi-k3
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter/Go rides served a model different from the one requested (sleep/worker)"
              description: "1 OpenRouter/Go ride(s) on sleep requested deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash but the /generation harvest (or Go ledger) shows they were SERVED as moonshotai/kimi-k3. Check the launcher's resolved model and whether `model_family()` normalizes both ids before assuming a redirect — the PR#407 class is what this belt exists to catch. Chainless alias remapping (slot maps, failover ladders) multiplies the places this can happen. The claude-harness/subscription rail has its own belt (agent_model_drift on github-exporter); rides whose served side is ABSENT are RouterRunModelUnverifiable, not this alert."
      # the roll (last sample 40m, first of the new pod at 43m) — the bridge holds
      - eval_time: 50m
        alertname: RouterRunModelDrift
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              rail: openrouter
              stack: sleep
              role: worker
              requested: deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash
              served: moonshotai/kimi-k3
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter/Go rides served a model different from the one requested (sleep/worker)"
              description: "1 OpenRouter/Go ride(s) on sleep requested deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash but the /generation harvest (or Go ledger) shows they were SERVED as moonshotai/kimi-k3. Check the launcher's resolved model and whether `model_family()` normalizes both ids before assuming a redirect — the PR#407 class is what this belt exists to catch. Chainless alias remapping (slot maps, failover ladders) multiplies the places this can happen. The claude-harness/subscription rail has its own belt (agent_model_drift on github-exporter); rides whose served side is ABSENT are RouterRunModelUnverifiable, not this alert."

  # ── D2 — the UNVERIFIABLE count is its own alert, and absent evidence is never agreement ──────
  # An OpenRouter ride requested tencent/hy3 with no /generation record in the store. This is NOT
  # drift (we cannot know what served it) — it is unverifiable, a distinct count with a distinct
  # alert, so a blind harvest can never read as a healthy run.
  - interval: 1m
    name: D2 unverifiable — a ride with no served evidence is its own count, never agreement
    input_series:
      - series: 'router_run_model_unverifiable_total{container="proxy",endpoint="http",instance="10.244.3.17:8080",job="openrouter-proxy",namespace="agent-egress",pod="openrouter-proxy-ttt-20",service="openrouter-proxy",rail="openrouter",stack="circles",role="worker",requested="tencent/hy3"}'
        values: "1+0x40"
    alert_rule_test:
      - eval_time: 29m
        alertname: RouterRunModelUnverifiable
        exp_alerts: []
      - eval_time: 31m
        alertname: RouterRunModelUnverifiable
        exp_alerts:
          - exp_labels:
              severity: warning
              rail: openrouter
              stack: circles
              role: worker
              requested: tencent/hy3
            exp_annotations:
              summary: "OpenRouter/Go rides have NO served-side evidence (circles/worker)"
              description: "1 OpenRouter/Go ride(s) on circles requested tencent/hy3 with NO served-side record in the router store for 30m — a /generation harvest miss, a Go ledger gap, or a ride that never produced telemetry. Absence never reads as agreement (the FU-108/FU-125 silent-success class): the belt is blind to what served them. Check openrouter_generation_harvest_total{outcome=\"missed\"} (a rising ratio = the harvest is half-blind) before assuming the rides were healthy."
